    "https://jobs.roosevelt.edu/postings/8912": {
      "title": "Student Systems and Process Improvement Specialist",
      "description": "Roosevelt University Employment Opportunities | Student Systems and Process Improvement Specialist Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help New Users Returning Users Student Systems and Process Improvement Specialist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Title Student Systems and Process Improvement Specialist Category Position Number Posting Number S0001402 Location Hybrid - Chicago Open Date 09/29/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled? Yes Job Type Full-Time Working Hours Position Summary The Student Systems and Process Improvement Specialist plays a central role in advancing Roosevelt University’s student success and operational excellence goals by leading the deployment of new registrar software systems and strengthening university data reporting and analytics capacity. Approximately half the position focuses on software project management—planning, coordinating, and overseeing the implementation and integration of new registrar technologies, and successful adoption and the effective use of AI. The remaining portion supports building and maintaining reports, analyzing data discrepancies, data quality, data definitions, and ensuring reliable, actionable information for decision-making. Roosevelt University offers many great benefits. Please review our benefits in further detail by clicking this link: https://rooseveltu.touchpointsonline.com/ The salary range for this position is $80,000-$82,500 Title IX Contact Information: Federal Title IX policy requires that all colleges and universities make known the contact information for the person responsible for coordinating its efforts to comply with Title IX regulations. At Roosevelt University, the Title IX Coordinator is Natasha Robinson. Her office is located in the Auditorium Building of the Chicago campus, 430 S. Michigan Avenue, AUD 476B. She can be reached at 312-281-3245 or TitleIX@roosevelt.edu. Do not contact this email address for inquiries about your job application; if you have questions, you may email hrquestions@roosevelt.edu . Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required Minimum of three years hands-on project management leadership experience in a higher education environment on multiple large-scale projects. Experience on the functional side of at least one major system of any Ellucian Banner system. Knowledge of the design, development and maintenance of reports and dashboards using Argos, EAB Edify, other analytics platforms and AI. Collaboration with departments to translate business questions into data requirements and produce actionable insights. Maintain documentation, metadata definitions, and data quality standards. Facilitation of data governance. The building and maintenance of reports, analyzing data discrepancies, data quality, data definitions and ensuring reliable, actionable information for decision-making. Exceptional communication skills and the ability to communicate appropriately at all levels of the organization; this includes written and verbal communications as well as visualizations. Years of Experience Required 3-5 years of experience in higher education Required Education Bachelor's Degree Certification/Licensure Required Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s Degree Working in a Higher Education environment Ellucian Banner experience Project Management experience Data report creation, maintenance, documentation, and governance Preferred Qualifications Equipment Operated Basic office equipment (desktop computer, copier/ multi-function device, fax and telephone). Working Conditions Basic office equipment (desktop computer, copier/ multi-function device, fax and telephone). Normal climate-controlled office environment is provided. Job is largely sedentary, but there is a need to move about the office and buildings frequently. Occasional bending, stooping, lifting, pushing and/or pulling are required (typically less than 10 pounds of weight or pressure). Regular use of desktop computers is required, calling for frequent hand/wrist movement and repetition. Moderate level of stress. Occasional travel within the local or regional setting may be required. Priority Application Instructions Departmental Contact Information Essential Job Functions Duty and Responsibility Registrar Software Deployment & Project Management: Serve as the project manager for new Registrar technology initiatives, leading projects from planning and requirements gathering through implementation, assessment, and ongoing improvement while ensuring alignment with institutional needs. Develop and maintain project plans, timelines, milestones, deliverables, decision logs, risk and issue tracking, testing plans, and status communications. Coordinate the configuration, integration, testing, and implementation of Registrar software with Banner and other enterprise systems, incorporating AI-enabled capabilities when appropriate and aligned with institutional standards. Facilitate user acceptance testing ( UAT ), develop documentation, training materials, standard operating procedures, and knowledge-transfer resources. Support change management, stakeholder communication, training, and adoption of new systems and processes. Percentage Of Time 50 Duty and Responsibility Reporting & Analytics: Design, develop, and maintain reports and dashboards using Argos, EAB Edify, other analytics platforms, and AI-enabled tools when appropriate. Collaborate with departments to translate business questions into data requirements and produce actionable insights. Develop and Maintain documentation, data definitions, data quality standards, and reporting procedures while ensuring accuracy, consistency, and attention to detail across institutional data sources. Perform detailed analysis of data discrepancies and system issues, identify root causes, and collaborate with stakeholders to implement sustainable solutions. Facilitate data governance for student data through policies, processes, and controls that support data security, integrity, usability, and availability. Partner with Registrar staff to create routine and ad hoc reports that identify data discrepancies, compliance issues, and operational inefficiencies. Train end users to access, interpret, and apply reporting tools effectively. Percentage Of Time 25 Duty and Responsibility Process Improvement & Integration Support: Analyze existing Registrar business processes and lead process-improvement initiatives by identifying and implementing AI-enabled and technology-driven solutions that improve efficiency, effectiveness, and user experience. Identify opportunities to streamline workflows, reduce errors, strengthen compliance, and improve service through process redesign and the responsible use of AI. Collaborate effectively with colleagues across departments, demonstrating a team-oriented approach to problem-solving, project execution, and continuous improvement. Maintain clear project and system documentation to support accountability, continuity, audit readiness, and knowledge sharing. Contribute to IT and Registrar strategic planning by recommending emerging technologies, AI opportunities, and reporting solutions. Percentage Of Time 25 Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have previous experience working in Ellucian/ Banner? Yes No Will you now or at any point in the future require employment visa sponsorship (e.g., H-1B) to legally work in the United States? Yes No Do you have 3-5 years of experience in higher education? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents 430 S. Michigan, Chicago, IL To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.roosevelt.edu/postings/8906": {
      "title": "Regional Admission Counselor",
      "description": "Roosevelt University Employment Opportunities | Regional Admission Counselor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help New Users Returning Users Regional Admission Counselor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Title Regional Admission Counselor Category Administrative Position Number Posting Number S0001400 Location Remote - Chicago Open Date 09/25/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled? Yes Job Type Full-Time Working Hours Position Summary Do you love discussing goals and possibilities with students? Do you have an entrepreneurial spirit? Are you interested in building a professional network at schools across the Midwest? If so, Roosevelt University is seeking a Regional Admission Counselor who will strengthen our partnerships throughout the Midwest, with a focus on recruitment in Indiana, Michigan, and Ohio. Roosevelt University has a social justice foundation, and our admissions team strives towards equity and creating access for students of all backgrounds. This position reports to the Director of First-Year Admission. This is a remote position with an expectation of consistent regional travel for recruitment, and there is an expectation that the Counselor will attend events and meetings at our Chicago campus a few times per year. The ideal candidate would reside in, or close to, the state of Indiana. The Regional Admission Counselor’s primary responsibility is to guide prospective students and families through the application process, which includes meeting with prospective first-year and transfer students, families, and school partners to assess academic eligibility, discuss academic offerings, and inform individuals of the Roosevelt student experience. The Counselor will manage an assigned territory of partner schools, serving as the primary point of contact for assigned school partners. The Counselor will travel and attend in-person or virtual events to recruit prospective students and will review applications for admission. Counselors also serve as an initial point of contact for post-admission processes to supplement other offices like Financial Aid Services, Academic Advising, or other student support services to ensure successful matriculation of transfer students. Please note that remote work is possible in the following states: AZ CA IL IN FL MN NC NE NY SC SD WA WI Roosevelt University offers many great benefits. Please review our benefits in further detail by clicking this link: https://rooseveltu.touchpointsonline.com/ The salary range for this position is $50,000-$52,000 Title IX Contact Information: Federal Title IX policy requires that all colleges and universities make known the contact information for the person responsible for coordinating its efforts to comply with Title IX regulations. At Roosevelt University, the Title IX Coordinator is Natasha Robinson. Her office is located in the Auditorium Building of the Chicago campus, 430 S. Michigan Avenue, AUD 476B. She can be reached at 312-281-3245 or TitleIX@roosevelt.edu. Do not contact this email address for inquiries about your job application; if you have questions, you may email hrquestions@roosevelt.edu . Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required A bachelor’s degree in relevant field. A minimum of 1-year experience working in admissions/enrollment. Strong public speaking and presentation skills. Ability to adapt communication style to different audiences. Ability to manage relationships both externally and within Roosevelt University. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relations in a multicultural environment. Ability to navigate personal and potentially difficult conversations and situations with care and tact. Ability to exercise considerable judgment and discretion when working with prospective students, family and school partners. Self-starter and ability to prioritize independently. Excellent organizational, communication (written and verbal), interpersonal skills and attention to detail. A positive and professional demeanor. Ability to demonstrate a commitment to service excellence by embodying Roosevelt’s core values, mission, and vision in every aspect of the role working with students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Experience with database systems like a CRM or SIS to input and access individualized and general information related to recruitment and enrollment . Demonstrates proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite including Word, Excel, and Outlook. Years of Experience Required 2 Required Education Bachelor's Degree Certification/Licensure Required Valid driver’s license and means to travel for recruitment. Minimum Qualifications See Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Required section. Preferred Qualifications Experience with Slate or another CRM . Experience with Banner or another SIS . Resides in the state of Indiana. Equipment Operated Basic office equipment: desktop/laptop computer, copier/ multi-function device, fax, cellphone and landline telephone. Software systems: CRM (Slate), Student Information System (Banner), video conferencing (Zoom) and remote communication tool (Microsoft Teams) Working Conditions Remote, with regional travel. The Counselor will work from home, and will travel to their assigned partner institutions regularly. Priority Application Instructions Departmental Contact Information Essential Job Functions Duty and Responsibility Recruitment and Territory Management: Understands and promotes the programs and services offered that contribute to the value and mission of Roosevelt University. Works with Director of First-Year Admission to develop out-of-state markets for Roosevelt University, especially in Indiana, Michigan, Wisconsin, and Ohio. Identifies, recruits, and admits prospective students in a designated market. Manages a recruitment territory from point of inquiry to matriculation. Executes and evaluates recruitment strategies. Conducts outreach by email, phone, or virtually with students, parents and school partners. Participates and/or assists with campus recruitment events and visit opportunities including weekend and evening hours. Relationship Building: Advises prospective applicants on the admissions process and opportunities at the University. Provides one-on-one and group admissions counseling to diverse populations – admission counseling occurs primarily by in-person or virtual appointments, phone calls, and email. Communicates requirements, expectations, opportunities and success stories with school partners to help support and identify potential students. Application Management Manages the applicant pool for all undergraduate programs in the assigned territory. Uses CRM (Slate), personal outreach, and other tools to facilitate application completion. Thoroughly reviews student credentials in making admission decisions using University guidelines and awarding merit scholarship opportunities. Uses the application and transcripts to evaluate students’ ability to succeed. Student Guidance Guides students through the enrollment process, from inquiry or application through matriculation. Provides guidance and pre-advising to prospective students to determine admissibility, appropriate program, location, and resources. Informs students of the various offerings and opportunities available while considering students’ abilities and needs to be successful.  In some cases, one may need to suggest other opportunities aside from selecting Roosevelt University if the decision is not beneficial to the student.  This could include suggesting attendance at a community college or additional coursework at a previous 4-year institution. Assists students with accessing and understanding general post-admission information and processes like financial aid, residence life, advising and student support services. Percentage Of Time 45 Duty and Responsibility Travel: Plans and arranges all phases of local and regional travel. Travels to local or regional high schools, community colleges, or other sites for recruitment events. Participates in off-campus events, visit opportunities or meetings to engage with prospective students, families and other partners. Collects and enters prospective student contact information for the CRM system. Percentage Of Time 45 Duty and Responsibility Other duties as assigned. Percentage Of Time 10 Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Do you have an earned Bachelor degree? yes no Do you have a valid driver's license? Yes No Will you now or at any point in the future require employment visa sponsorship (e.g., H-1B) to legally work in the United States? Yes No Do you have a minimum of 1-year experience working in admissions/enrollment? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents 430 S. Michigan, Chicago, IL To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.roosevelt.edu/postings/8903": {
      "title": "Career Services Manager, Internships &amp; Career Programs",
      "description": "Roosevelt University Employment Opportunities | Career Services Manager, Internships &amp; Career Programs Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help New Users Returning Users Career Services Manager, Internships & Career Programs Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Title Career Services Manager, Internships & Career Programs Category Administrative Position Number Posting Number S0001399 Location Hybrid - Chicago Open Date 09/24/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled? Yes Job Type Full-Time Working Hours Position Summary The Career Services Manager, Internships & Career Programs coordinates and advances internship, experiential learning, employer engagement, and career programming initiatives. Working in collaboration with the Director of Career Services, the Career Services Manager develops and administers career-connected learning opportunities, maintains relationships with employers and faculty, and delivers programs that prepare undergraduate and graduate students for internships, employment, graduate education, and other post-graduation opportunities. The position also serves as the primary Career Services liaison to the Chicago College of Performing Arts ( CCPA ), provides targeted career advising, supervises assigned student and/or graduate employees, and contributes to departmental and University wide career readiness initiatives. The role manages an assigned portfolio of programs, partnerships, and operational processes within established departmental priorities, policies, and goals, while recommending improvements in collaboration with the Director of Career Services. Roosevelt University offers many great benefits. Please review our benefits in further detail by clicking this link: https://rooseveltu.touchpointsonline.com/ The salary range for this position is $60,000-$62,000 Title IX Contact Information: Federal Title IX policy requires that all colleges and universities make known the contact information for the person responsible for coordinating its efforts to comply with Title IX regulations. At Roosevelt University, the Title IX Coordinator is Natasha Robinson. Her office is located in the Auditorium Building of the Chicago campus, 430 S. Michigan Avenue, AUD 476B. She can be reached at 312-281-3245 or TitleIX@roosevelt.edu. Do not contact this email address for inquiries about your job application; if you have questions, you may email hrquestions@roosevelt.edu . Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required Knowledge of career development, career readiness, internships, experiential learning, employer engagement, recruiting, and workforce development practices. Strong relationship development, networking, employer outreach, and partnership-management skills. Ability to understand employer talent needs and connect those needs with students, academic programs, and University resources. Ability to manage and coordinate programs and partnerships from planning through implementation, administration, and assessment. Strong career advising, communication, presentation, and facilitation skills. Ability to exercise sound professional judgment, manage multiple priorities, and work effectively both independently and collaboratively Ability to use data and feedback to evaluate programs and recommend improvements. Ability to supervise and support student and graduate-level employees. Ability to work effectively with students from diverse academic programs, backgrounds, identities, and career interests. Proficiency with Handshake, LinkedIn, and other career-related technologies. Ability to represent Roosevelt University professionally with internal and external stakeholders. Years of Experience Required 2-4 Required Education Bachelor's Degree Certification/Licensure Required Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in higher education, student affairs, counseling, education, business, communications, human resources, marketing, arts administration, or a related field. Two to four years of progressively responsible professional experience in career services, internships, experiential learning, employer relations, higher education, workforce development, business development, recruiting, or a related area. Experience managing or independently coordinating internship, experiential learning, career readiness, employer engagement, or career development programs and partnerships. Experience building and maintaining relationships with employers, faculty, academic leadership, alumni, community partners, professional associations, or other stakeholders. Experience working directly with college students through career advising, coaching, professional development, internship support, or related student services. Experience developing and facilitating programs, workshops, presentations, or educational experiences. Strong communication, presentation, organizational, relationship-management, and project-coordination skills. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, exercise professional judgment, work independently within established goals, and collaborate across organizational areas. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in higher education, student affairs, counseling, education, organizational leadership, business, human resources, marketing, arts administration, or a related field. Experience developing employer partnerships through outreach, business development, recruiting, or partnership development. Experience coordinating internship or experiential learning initiatives in higher education. Knowledge of the NACE Career Readiness Competencies and internship best practices. Experience supervising student employees, graduate assistants, or interns. Experience using Handshake or a comparable career management platform. Experience with program assessment, employer engagement data, grants, sponsorships, or externally funded initiatives. Experience working with performing arts students, creative industries, arts organizations, or other specialized student populations. Equipment Operated Working Conditions Normal climate-controlled office environment is provided. Occasional bending, lifting, pushing and/or pulling is required (typically less than 10 lbs of weight). Regular use of desktop computer is required calling for frequent hand/wrist movement and repetition. Moderate level of stress. Priority Application Instructions Departmental Contact Information Essential Job Functions Duty and Responsibility Internships & Experiential Learning: Manage and coordinate the development, implementation, administration, and ongoing improvement of internship and experiential learning programs for undergraduate and graduate students. Develop and administer career-connected experiences beyond traditional internships, including micro-internships, project-based experiences, career treks, employer site visits, job shadowing, and other applied learning opportunities. Collaborate with faculty and academic leadership to connect internship and experiential learning opportunities with academic programs and student career outcomes. Manage, maintain, and improve assigned processes, resources, guidelines, and communications that support effective internship and experiential learning experiences. Serve as a knowledgeable resource for students, faculty, staff, and employers regarding internship and experiential learning practices, policies, resources, and opportunities. Monitor participation, student needs, and program outcomes to identify barriers, recommend improvements, and strengthen access and program quality. Research potential grants, employer funding, sponsorships, and other resources, and assist with proposals or implementation as assigned. Manage and coordinate communication and promotion of experiential learning opportunities through Handshake, faculty partnerships, employer relationships, and University communication channels. Participate in University committees, initiatives, and cross-functional efforts related to internships, experiential learning, and career-connected learning Percentage Of Time 30 Duty and Responsibility Employer Partnerships: Develop and maintain employer and industry relationships that expand career opportunities, talent pipelines, and engagement for Roosevelt students and alumni. Conduct proactive employer outreach through professional networks, industry associations, alumni connections, employer visits, LinkedIn, referrals, and targeted prospecting. Meet with employers to understand workforce needs, recruitment strategies, early-career talent priorities, and opportunities for collaboration. Manage an assigned portfolio of employer and industry partners, maintain regular communication, and identify opportunities for continued engagement. Manage and coordinate employer engagement activities, including classroom visits, information sessions, networking, mentoring, career fairs, site visits, career treks, sponsorships, and other talent-development initiatives. Connect employer talent needs with students, academic programs, faculty, alumni, and Career Services resources to strengthen recruiting and career pathways. Maintain accurate employer, partnership, opportunity, and engagement data in Handshake and other University systems. Review employer feedback, recruiting trends, and labor-market information to support career programming, student preparation, and recommendations for partnership development. Represent Roosevelt University and Career Services in employer meetings, professional associations, networking events, community engagements, and other approved external activities. Percentage Of Time 25 Duty and Responsibility Career Programs & Professional Development: Manage, coordinate, and deliver assigned career readiness programs for undergraduate and graduate students through workshops, classroom presentations, panels, networking opportunities, mock interviews, and other career-focused educational experiences. Provide programming related to resume and cover letter development, interviewing, networking, LinkedIn, internship and job searching, professional communication, personal branding, and workplace preparation. Incorporate the NACE Career Readiness Competencies into assigned Career Services programming and experiential learning initiatives. Help students identify and communicate skills developed through academic, employment, internship, leadership, and co-curricular experiences. Partner with employers and alumni to introduce students to industries, career pathways, workplace expectations, and professional opportunities. Collaborate with faculty to integrate career readiness and professional development into appropriate academic experiences. Develop and maintain scalable career education resources that students can access independently through Handshake and other University platforms. Percentage Of Time 20 Duty and Responsibility Career Advising & CCPA Liaison: Provide targeted career advising primarily to CCPA students and students seeking specialized internship or experiential learning support. Advise students on internship and job searches, resumes and cover letters, interviewing, networking, LinkedIn, career exploration, and professional preparation. Serve as the primary Career Services contact for CCPA students, faculty, staff, and academic leadership. Partner with CCPA faculty and leadership to identify and respond to the career development, internship, employer engagement, and professional preparation needs of CCPA students. Manage and coordinate CCPA -specific career programs, workshops, industry connections, and professional development opportunities. Cultivate relationships with employers, alumni, professional associations, and organizations within the arts, entertainment, cultural, nonprofit, and creative sectors to expand opportunities and industry connections for CCPA students. Percentage Of Time 15 Duty and Responsibility Program Coordination, Assessment & Department Support: Develop annual goals and work plans for assigned internship, experiential learning, employer engagement, and career programming responsibilities in collaboration with the Director of Career Services. Independently manage and coordinate assigned programs, initiatives, and partnerships within established priorities, and recommend improvements based on participation, feedback, and outcomes. Maintain assessment measures related to internship activity, employer engagement, student participation, learning, and career outcomes. Use data and feedback from students, employers, and faculty to evaluate effectiveness and recommend program, service, and partnership improvements. Prepare reports, summaries, presentations, and recommendations related to assigned functional areas and departmental priorities. Monitor timelines, deliverables, partner commitments, and operational details across assigned programs and communicate progress, risks, and recommendations to the Director of Career Services. Supervise, train, schedule, assign work to, and provide feedback to assigned undergraduate student employees and/or graduate assistants. Participate in Career Services and University committees, working groups, and external partnerships as assigned. Contribute to departmental projects, office coverage, and operational needs in coordination with the Director of Career Services; this position does not assume departmental leadership or the Director’s supervisory authority in the Director’s absence. Maintain knowledge of current trends and best practices in career services, internships, experiential learning, employer engagement, early-career recruiting, and workforce development. Perform other related duties consistent with the scope and level of the position. Percentage Of Time 10 Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Will you now or at any point in the future require employment visa sponsorship (e.g., H-1B) to legally work in the United States? Yes No Do you have a Bachelor’s degree in higher education, student affairs, counseling, education, business, communications, human resources, marketing, arts administration, or a related field? Yes No Do you have two to four years of progressively responsible professional experience in career services, internships, experiential learning, employer relations, higher education, workforce development, business development, recruiting, or a related area? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents 430 S. Michigan, Chicago, IL To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
