    "https://jobs.roosevelt.edu/postings/8940": {
      "title": "Disabilities Services Specialist",
      "description": "Roosevelt University Employment Opportunities | Disabilities Services Specialist Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help New Users Returning Users Disabilities Services Specialist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Title Disabilities Services Specialist Category Administrative Position Number A99408 Posting Number S0001409 Location Hybrid - Chicago Open Date 10/15/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled? Yes Job Type Full-Time Working Hours Position Summary The Disabilities Services Specialist provides direct support to students with disabilities by reviewing documentation, evaluating accommodation requests, coordinating approved accommodations, and connecting students with appropriate University resources. The Specialist works collaboratively with students, faculty, and staff to support equitable access to academic programs, services, and activities. The Specialist promotes student success, self-advocacy, engagement, and belonging through individualized support, campus partnerships, educational outreach, and student-centered programming. This includes establishing and facilitating opportunities for students with disabilities to build community, develop peer connections, and share feedback about their campus experiences. The Specialist supports the implementation of the University’s disability-related policies and procedures within the scope of student services. The position does not serve as the University’s designated ADA /Section 504 Coordinator and does not carry institution-wide compliance oversight or employee accommodation responsibilities. Occasional evening and weekend work may be required. Roosevelt University offers many great benefits. Please review our benefits in further detail by clicking this link: https://rooseveltu.touchpointsonline.com/ The salary range for this position is $55,000-$57,800 Title IX Contact Information: Federal Title IX policy requires that all colleges and universities make known the contact information for the person responsible for coordinating its efforts to comply with Title IX regulations. At Roosevelt University, the Title IX Coordinator is Natasha Robinson. Her office is located in the Auditorium Building of the Chicago campus, 430 S. Michigan Avenue, AUD 476B. She can be reached at 312-281-3245 or TitleIX@roosevelt.edu. Do not contact this email address for inquiries about your job application; if you have questions, you may email hrquestions@roosevelt.edu . Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required Working knowledge of the Americans with Disabilities Act ( ADA ), Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, and their application to disability accommodations in higher education. Ability to review and interpret disability-related documentation and apply established procedures to accommodation requests. Demonstrated ability to exercise sound judgment, discretion, and professionalism when working with confidential and sensitive information. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to establish effective working relationships with students, faculty, staff, and campus partners. Excellent written communication skills, including the ability to maintain clear, accurate, and confidential case notes. Strong oral communication and presentation skills. Ability to work collaboratively as part of a team while managing assigned responsibilities independently. Commitment to student-centered service, accessibility, inclusion, and the success of students with diverse backgrounds and experiences. Strong organizational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities and student requests. Proficiency with Microsoft Outlook, Excel, and Word. Years of Experience Required Three years of relevant professional experience with a bachelor's degree, or one year of relevant professional experience with a master's degree. Required Education Bachelor's Degree Certification/Licensure Required Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in special education, rehabilitation counseling, psychology, social work, higher education, or a related field. Three years of relevant professional experience with a bachelor’s degree, or one year of relevant professional experience with a master’s degree. Experience providing individualized support, advising, or case management services to students or individuals with disabilities. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in special education, rehabilitation counseling, psychology, higher education, or a related field. Experience providing disability-related accommodations or support services in a college or university setting. Experience using accommodation management systems, such as AIM or similar platforms. Experience reviewing disability documentation and participating in individualized accommodation processes. Familiarity with assistive technology and accessible educational resources. Experience developing or facilitating student engagement programs, peer networks, workshops, or other community-building initiatives. Experience collaborating with faculty and campus partners to support students with disabilities. Equipment Operated Standard office equipment, including computers and telephones. Familiarity with assistive technology, adaptive equipment, and accommodation management software is preferred. Working Conditions Work is performed primarily in a climate-controlled office environment and involves regular computer use, student meetings, and collaboration with faculty and staff. The position may require movement between campus offices and buildings, as well as occasional evening or weekend work. Reasonable accommodations will be provided to qualified applicants and employees in accordance with applicable law. Priority Application Instructions Departmental Contact Information Essential Job Functions Duty and Responsibility Direct Student Accommodation Services: Review and evaluate student disability documentation, including medical, psychological, and other relevant evaluations, to assess accommodation requests in accordance with applicable laws and University procedures. Engage in an individualized, interactive process with students to identify disability-related barriers and determine appropriate reasonable accommodations within established University procedures and delegated authority. Coordinate the implementation of approved accommodations with students, faculty, and appropriate University offices. Consult with students regarding their accommodation needs, available resources, and strategies for accessing academic programs and services. Provide ongoing support to students experiencing disability-related barriers to academic participation and success. Assist students in developing self-advocacy skills and understanding how to request, access, and use approved accommodations. Support students in identifying appropriate assistive technologies, accessibility resources, and learning strategies. Coordinate accommodated testing arrangements with faculty and appropriate campus partners, consistent with approved accommodations and established procedures. Maintain accurate, confidential student records, accommodation documentation, and case notes in the University’s designated accommodation management system. Refer students to appropriate University and community resources when additional support is needed. Percentage Of Time 60 Duty and Responsibility Faculty Consultation, Education, and Reporting: Provide consultation and guidance to faculty and staff regarding the implementation of approved student accommodations. Collaborate with academic departments and campus partners to identify and address barriers to students’ access and participation. Develop and deliver workshops, presentations, and educational resources focused on disability awareness, accessibility, inclusion, and student self-advocacy. Provide information to faculty and staff regarding Disability Services procedures, resources, and responsibilities. Maintain current knowledge of disability-related laws, professional standards, emerging practices, and resources relevant to postsecondary education. Contribute to the assessment of Disability Services programs, including identifying student needs, service trends, and opportunities for improvement. Prepare and maintain service data, reports, and other documentation to support program evaluation and planning Percentage Of Time 20 Duty and Responsibility Student Engagement and Program Coordination: Develop and implement student-centered programs and initiatives that promote engagement, belonging, self-advocacy, and academic success among students with disabilities. Establish and facilitate an informal student disability community or peer network that provides opportunities for students to connect, build relationships, share experiences, and offer feedback on accessibility and campus life. Encourage student participation and leadership in identifying programming interests and opportunities for community building. Collaborate with Student Affairs, the Learning Commons, and other campus partners to promote inclusive and accessible student engagement opportunities. Identify emerging student needs and recommend improvements to programs, services, and resources. Support initiatives that strengthen students’ connection to the University and their ability to participate fully in campus life. Collaborate with the Learning Commons team and other University partners on proposals and initiatives that expand student support and accessibility. Perform other duties as assigned that are consistent with the scope and responsibilities of the position. Percentage Of Time 20 Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Do you have a Bachelor's degree in special education, rehabilitation counseling, psychology, social work, higher education, or a related field? Yes No Do you have three years of relevant professional experience with a bachelor's degree, or one year of relevant professional experience with a master's degree? Yes No Will you now or at any point in the future require employment visa sponsorship (e.g., H-1B) to legally work in the United States? Yes No This is a hybrid position. Do you have the ability to work on the Chicago campus 3 days per week and attend any other in-person events as required? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents 430 S. Michigan, Chicago, IL To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.roosevelt.edu/postings/8937": {
      "title": "Admission Counselor, Chicago College of Performing Arts",
      "description": "Roosevelt University Employment Opportunities | Admission Counselor, Chicago College of Performing Arts Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help New Users Returning Users Admission Counselor, Chicago College of Performing Arts Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Title Admission Counselor, Chicago College of Performing Arts Category Administrative Position Number Posting Number S0001408 Location Hybrid - Chicago Open Date 10/15/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled? No Job Type Full-Time Working Hours Position Summary The Admission Counselor for the Chicago College of Performing Arts’ primary responsibility is to guide students and families through the application, audition, and enrollment process, which includes meeting with prospective students, families, and faculty to assess fit and eligibility, discuss academic and artistic offerings, and inform individuals of the Roosevelt/ CCPA student experience. The counselor will travel and attend in-person and/or virtual events to recruit prospective students and review applications for admission, as well as organize and execute admission audition scheduling. The counselor will work closely with CCPA faculty and is responsible for the public face of the recruitment efforts for the Chicago College of Performing Arts ( CCPA ). The counselor is one of three staff members responsible for recruitment to the Interdisciplinary, Music, or Theatre Conservatory programs currently offered, recruitment event travel, and will oversee the application process for 200 to 700 of our applicants. CCPA Enrollment Staff also serve as an initial point of contact for post-admission processes to supplement other offices like Financial Aid Services, Housing & Residence Life, Academic Advising, or other student support services. The Admission Counselor is encouraged to work collaboratively and independently in developing strategies specific to the counselor’s assigned areas (of the college’s performing arts programs) and overall matriculation of admitted students. The modality of this position is hybrid, with an average of 3 days onsite (Chicago campus) per week. Travel is required. Some evenings and weekends are required. This is a full-time, exempt position reporting to the Assistant Dean of Enrollment Management in the Chicago College of Performing Arts We are committed to diversity, equity, and inclusion, and we encourage candidates from all backgrounds to apply. Roosevelt University offers many great benefits. Please review our benefits in further detail by clicking this link: https://rooseveltu.touchpointsonline.com/ The salary range for this position is $49,500-$51,000 Title IX Contact Information: Federal Title IX policy requires that all colleges and universities make known the contact information for the person responsible for coordinating its efforts to comply with Title IX regulations. At Roosevelt University, the Title IX Coordinator is Natasha Robinson. Her office is located in the Auditorium Building of the Chicago campus, 430 S. Michigan Avenue, AUD 476B. She can be reached at 312-281-3245 or TitleIX@roosevelt.edu. Do not contact this email address for inquiries about your job application; if you have questions, you may email hrquestions@roosevelt.edu . Knowledge, Skills and Abilities Required A bachelor’s degree in a relevant field – preferably music, theatre, performing arts, arts administration, or a related field from a Music or Theatre Conservatory or School. A minimum of 1-year experience working in college admissions/enrollment, student affairs, higher education, or performing arts administration. The ability to travel, drive, and recruit is essential. Strong public speaking and presentation skills. Ability to adapt communication style to different audiences. Ability to manage relationships both externally and within Roosevelt University. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relations in a multicultural environment. Ability to manage overall team event calendar, as well as personal recruitment calendar. Ability to navigate personal and potentially difficult conversations and situations with care and tact. Ability to exercise considerable judgment and discretion when working with prospective students, families, and school partners. Self-starter and ability to prioritize independently. Excellent organizational, communication (written and verbal), interpersonal skills and attention to detail. A positive and professional demeanor. Ability to demonstrate a commitment to service excellence by embodying Roosevelt’s core values, mission, and vision in every aspect of the role working with students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Experience with database systems like a CRM or SIS to input and access individualized and general information related to recruitment and enrollment. Demonstrated proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite including Word, Excel, and Outlook. Years of Experience Required 1 Required Education Bachelor's Degree Certification/Licensure Required Valid driver’s license and/or means to travel for recruitment. Minimum Qualifications A Bachelor’s degree is required. A minimum of 1 year experience of higher education admission/enrollment management and/or student services. Please refer to knowledge, skills, and abilities required. Preferred Qualifications Performance and/or Academic Background in Music, Performing Arts, Performing Arts Administration. Experience working in a music or theatre conservatory/school. Equipment Operated Basic office equipment: desktop/laptop computer, copier/ multi-function device, fax, cellphone, and landline telephone. Software systems: CRM (Slate), video conferencing (Zoom), artistic application and scheduling site Acceptd, and remote communication tool (Microsoft Teams) Working Conditions Normal climate-controlled office environment is provided. Job is often sedentary, but there is a need to move about the office and buildings frequently. Occasional bending, stooping, lifting, pushing and/or pulling is required (typically less than 10 pounds of weight or pressure). Regular use of desktop computer is required, calling for frequent hand/wrist movement and repetition. Some evening and weekend hours may be required as necessary for both onsite and offsite events. Traveling by car, public transit, or flight may be required as necessary to travel to and from event sites. Moderate level of stress. Priority Application Instructions Departmental Contact Information Essential Job Functions Duty and Responsibility Recruitment and Application Management: Understand and promote the programs offered that contribute to the value and mission of CCPA and Roosevelt University. Identify, recruit, and admit prospective students in a designated market. Manage a recruitment territory from inquiry to matriculation. Execute and evaluate recruitment strategies. Conduct outreach by email, phone or virtually with students, parents and school partners. Participate in, schedule, manage on-campus admission auditions, recruitment events, and visit opportunities including weekend and evening hours. Use CRM (Slate), GetAcceptd.com, outreach and other tools to facilitate application completion. Thoroughly review student credentials in making admission decisions using guidelines provided by the University and awarding merit scholarship opportunities. Use the application, transcripts, and audition/interview materials to evaluate students’ ability to succeed. Help maintain daily tour and student campus visit schedule with Enrollment Team. Be present physically on campus for all admission audition days for their assigned program areas (this includes weekdays, weekends, and evenings spread throughout the end of January and through February) and provide on-site support for full college events. Percentage Of Time 40 Duty and Responsibility Student Guidance: Guide students through the enrollment process, from inquiry or application through matriculation. Provide guidance and pre-advising to prospective students to determine admissibility, appropriate program, faculty connection, and resources. Inform students of the various offerings and opportunities available while considering students’ abilities and needs to be successful. Assist students with accessing and understanding general post-admission information and processes like financial aid, residence life, advising and student support services. Percentage Of Time 25 Duty and Responsibility Relationship Building: Counsel prospective applicants on the admissions process and opportunities at CCPA . Provide one-on-one and group admissions counseling to diverse populations – admission counseling occurs primarily by in-person or virtual appointments, campus tours, phone calls and email. Communicate requirements, expectations, opportunities and success stories with school partners to help support and identify potential students Actively engage in CCPA social media channels (FB and Instagram) to promote posted content to applicants, which attracts and engages prospective students and families. Percentage Of Time 20 Duty and Responsibility Travel: Plan and arrange all phases of their local and regional travel. Travel to local or regional high schools, colleges, or other sites for recruitment events. Participate in off-campus events, visit opportunities, or meetings to engage with prospective students, families and other partners. Collect and enter prospective student contact information for the CRM system. Percentage Of Time 15 Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a bachelor's degree? Yes No * Do you have a minimum of 1 year of experience working in college admission, student affairs, customer service, or related field? Yes No * Do you have a valid driver's license?. Yes No This is a hybrid position. Do you have the ability to work on the Chicago campus 3 days per week and attend any other in-person events as required? Yes No Will you now or at any point in the future require employment visa sponsorship (e.g., H-1B) to legally work in the United States? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents 430 S. Michigan, Chicago, IL To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
  },
